WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.071
Lesson one point two.

2
00:00:01.369 --> 00:00:03.302
How our drum model hears a guitar part.

3
00:00:07.243 --> 00:00:09.411
Here is one bar of guitar, at a hundred

4
00:00:09.411 --> 00:00:10.856
and twenty beats per minute.

5
00:00:11.166 --> 00:00:13.488
Each dot is an onset: the moment a note

6
00:00:13.488 --> 00:00:15.811
starts, wherever the guitarist happened to

7
00:00:15.811 --> 00:00:16.224
play it.

8
00:00:16.738 --> 00:00:18.533
We cut time into sixteenth notes.

9
00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:21.145
Sixty seconds, divided by the beats per

10
00:00:21.145 --> 00:00:23.593
minute, gives one beat: half a second.

11
00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:26.042
A beat holds four sixteenths, so one

12
00:00:26.042 --> 00:00:28.109
sixteenth lasts an eighth of a second.

13
00:00:28.436 --> 00:00:29.742
We call each one a slot.

14
00:00:30.211 --> 00:00:31.495
So one bar is sixteen slots.

15
00:00:32.151 --> 00:00:34.743
To place a note, measure how many slots it

16
00:00:34.743 --> 00:00:37.279
sits after the bar line, and round to the

17
00:00:37.279 --> 00:00:38.373
nearest whole slot.

18
00:00:38.719 --> 00:00:41.081
This note came at zero point seven seven

19
00:00:41.081 --> 00:00:41.542
seconds.

20
00:00:41.888 --> 00:00:44.365
That's six point one six slots in, so it

21
00:00:44.365 --> 00:00:45.345
goes in slot six.

22
00:00:45.794 --> 00:00:47.767
Every note snaps to its nearest slot.

23
00:00:48.087 --> 00:00:50.327
Now a slot either holds an onset, or it

24
00:00:50.327 --> 00:00:50.754
doesn't.

25
00:00:54.503 --> 00:00:56.654
Two bars side by side make thirty-two

26
00:00:56.654 --> 00:00:57.955
slots: one window.

27
00:00:58.294 --> 00:01:00.728
The marks above are the beats, four to a

28
00:01:00.728 --> 00:01:00.954
bar.

29
00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:05.774
The model gets five numbers for every

30
00:01:05.774 --> 00:01:06.054
slot.

31
00:01:06.389 --> 00:01:08.792
The first is the onset: one if a note

32
00:01:08.792 --> 00:01:10.805
started in this slot, zero if not.

33
00:01:11.287 --> 00:01:13.647
The second is velocity: how hard the note

34
00:01:13.647 --> 00:01:15.756
was hit, scaled from MIDI's nought to a

35
00:01:15.756 --> 00:01:17.915
hundred and twenty-seven down to nought to

36
00:01:17.915 --> 00:01:18.116
one.

37
00:01:18.983 --> 00:01:21.218
The third is intensity: how busy the

38
00:01:21.218 --> 00:01:22.442
guitar has been lately.

39
00:01:22.761 --> 00:01:23.985
It's a running average.

40
00:01:24.304 --> 00:01:26.805
Each slot, keep most of the old value, and

41
00:01:26.805 --> 00:01:28.667
add a small share of the new onset.

42
00:01:28.986 --> 00:01:31.274
The share, alpha, is chosen so that an

43
00:01:31.274 --> 00:01:33.668
onset's effect halves every sixteen slots,

44
00:01:33.668 --> 00:01:34.094
one bar.

45
00:01:34.577 --> 00:01:36.358
Here it is across the two bars.

46
00:01:36.702 --> 00:01:39.058
It starts at zero, because nothing has

47
00:01:39.058 --> 00:01:41.471
been heard yet, and climbs as the notes

48
00:01:41.471 --> 00:01:42.160
keep coming.

49
00:01:42.505 --> 00:01:44.917
One onset on its own adds just zero point

50
00:01:44.917 --> 00:01:45.492
zero four.

51
00:01:46.795 --> 00:01:49.063
The fourth is a flag: does this song's

52
00:01:49.063 --> 00:01:50.610
velocity mean anything at all?

53
00:01:50.919 --> 00:01:53.085
In seventeen percent of the rock songs,

54
00:01:53.085 --> 00:01:55.250
the notes use at most two loudness values.

55
00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:57.777
The model is told when the channel carries

56
00:01:57.777 --> 00:01:58.189
nothing.

57
00:01:58.499 --> 00:02:00.716
Live, the board's velocity isn't trusted

58
00:02:00.716 --> 00:02:02.881
yet, so the laptop sends one flat value

59
00:02:02.881 --> 00:02:04.015
with the flag at zero.

60
00:02:04.459 --> 00:02:06.882
And the fifth is tempo, shifted so that a

61
00:02:06.882 --> 00:02:08.369
hundred and twenty is zero.

62
00:02:08.699 --> 00:02:11.177
A hundred and eighty is plus a half, sixty

63
00:02:11.177 --> 00:02:12.058
is minus a half.

64
00:02:15.353 --> 00:02:16.333
There is one catch.

65
00:02:16.643 --> 00:02:18.707
The three guitar rows slide one slot to

66
00:02:18.707 --> 00:02:19.222
the right.

67
00:02:19.532 --> 00:02:21.853
So when the model decides slot t, it reads

68
00:02:21.853 --> 00:02:24.226
what the guitar did at slot t minus one:

69
00:02:24.226 --> 00:02:25.258
the model is causal.

70
00:02:25.568 --> 00:02:27.631
It can't use a note it hasn't heard yet.

71
00:02:27.941 --> 00:02:30.417
The flag and the tempo don't move: they're

72
00:02:30.417 --> 00:02:31.294
known in advance.

73
00:02:35.023 --> 00:02:35.825
Take slot zero.

74
00:02:36.145 --> 00:02:38.442
Its five numbers go into the drum model,

75
00:02:38.442 --> 00:02:40.739
and out come nine probabilities, one for

76
00:02:40.739 --> 00:02:41.273
each drum.

77
00:02:41.765 --> 00:02:44.345
A decoder turns them into hits: a drum

78
00:02:44.345 --> 00:02:46.515
plays if its probability clears that

79
00:02:46.515 --> 00:02:47.746
drum's own threshold.

80
00:02:48.098 --> 00:02:50.444
Each threshold is set so the drum plays

81
00:02:50.444 --> 00:02:52.496
about as often as it does in forty

82
00:02:52.496 --> 00:02:53.493
validation songs.

83
00:02:54.631 --> 00:02:55.386
Then slot one.

84
00:02:55.709 --> 00:02:56.463
Then slot two.

85
00:02:56.786 --> 00:02:59.157
Slot by slot, the drum part is written,

86
00:02:59.157 --> 00:03:01.419
always one sixteenth behind the guitar it

87
00:03:01.419 --> 00:03:01.958
has heard.

88
00:03:02.929 --> 00:03:05.067
Some of our models also read back the

89
00:03:05.067 --> 00:03:06.811
drums they played one slot ago.

90
00:03:07.149 --> 00:03:09.399
That's called option C, and it gets a

91
00:03:09.399 --> 00:03:10.412
lesson of its own.

92
00:03:11.987 --> 00:03:14.266
So the interface is small: five numbers

93
00:03:14.266 --> 00:03:16.242
in, nine out, every sixteenth note.

94
00:03:16.546 --> 00:03:18.573
Next: the loop that runs this live.

